Here's a complete example for a C# MCP server that wraps a REST API using stdio transport:

---

## Tool Class — `ApiTools.cs`

```csharp
using ModelContextProtocol.Server;
using System.ComponentModel;
using System.Text.Json;

[McpServerToolType]
public class ApiTools(HttpClient httpClient, ILogger<ApiTools> logger)
{
    [McpServerTool, Description("Get a user by their unique identifier.")]
    public async Task<string> GetUser(
        [Description("The user's numeric ID")] int userId,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        logger.LogInformation("Fetching user {UserId}", userId);
        var response = await httpClient.GetAsync($"/users/{userId}", cancellationToken);
        response.EnsureSuccessStatusCode();
        return await response.Content.ReadAsStringAsync(cancellationToken);
    }

    [McpServerTool, Description("Search users by name. Returns a JSON array of matches.")]
    public async Task<string> SearchUsers(
        [Description("Partial or full name to search for")] string query,
        [Description("Max results to return (1-50)")] int limit = 10,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        logger.LogInformation("Searching users: {Query}", query);
        var response = await httpClient.GetAsync(
            $"/users?name={Uri.EscapeDataString(query)}&limit={limit}", cancellationToken);
        response.EnsureSuccessStatusCode();
        return await response.Content.ReadAsStringAsync(cancellationToken);
    }
}
```

## `Program.cs` — stdio transport

```csharp
using Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging;

var builder = Host.CreateApplicationBuilder(args);

// CRITICAL: log to stderr only — stdout is the JSON-RPC channel
builder.Logging.AddConsole(options =>
    options.LogToStandardErrorThreshold = LogLevel.Trace);

// Register HttpClient with a base address for API tools
builder.Services.AddHttpClient<ApiTools>(client =>
{
    client.BaseAddress = new Uri("https://api.example.com");
});

builder.Services.AddMcpServer()
    .WithStdioServerTransport()
    .WithToolsFromAssembly();

await builder.Build().RunAsync();
```

---

## How DI Works for MCP Tools

The SDK integrates with `Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection` automatically:

| Mechanism | How it works |
|-----------|--------------|
| **Constructor injection** | Non-static `[McpServerToolType]` classes are resolved from DI. Use primary constructors (as above) to inject `HttpClient`, `ILogger<T>`, etc. |
| **Method parameter injection** | Any parameter whose type is registered in DI (and isn't a tool input) is injected automatically and **hidden from the tool schema**. |
| **Framework types** | `CancellationToken`, `IMcpServer`, `IProgress<ProgressNotificationValue>` are always injected — never exposed to the LLM. |

**Key rule:** Only parameters with `[Description]` appear in the tool's JSON schema. Everything else is treated as a service to inject.

### To scaffold this project from scratch:

```bash
dotnet new mcpserver -n MyApiServer
cd MyApiServer
# Replace the generated files with the code above
dotnet build && dotnet run
```

The server will start, listen on stdin for JSON-RPC messages, and tools will be discoverable by any MCP client (VS Code, GitHub Copilot, MCP Inspector, etc.).
